Make an answered COMMIT, rename or stable write mean the data is on the drive,
whatever the export says.

The macOS client writes UNSTABLE and then sends COMMIT, and takes the reply as
its data being durable. nfsd answers a COMMIT with vfs_fsync_range() over the
client's range, and on an async export with nothing. On ntfs3 a range fsync is
generic_file_fsync, which writes the file's pages and inode and not the
volume's own metadata, which only syncfs(2) writes. Measured on a real drive on
2026-09-10: after a 32 MiB write and fsync from the Mac, 464 kB was still dirty
in the guest; an fsync of the file cleared 208 kB of it, and syncfs of the
volume the other 256 kB.

COMMIT now syncs the file's filesystem as syncfs(2) does, with s_umount held
for reading, then flushes the device, so what the sync wrote is behind the
flush. A writeback error, the file's and then the filesystem's, is told to the
open file once, as fsync(2) and syncfs(2) tell it, and changes the write
verifier, so that the client writes again what it had been told was safe.

An async export spares every create and remove a sync of its own (29 ms and
10 ms each on the test drive, against none), but nfsd also made a stable write
unstable there while telling the client it was stable, and skipped a rename's
metadata commit. On an async export a stable write is now followed by the same
sync as a COMMIT, because the client sends no COMMIT after it and the create
before it was not synced either; on a sync export it stays the range fsync it
was. A rename on an async export syncs the filesystem, once its locks are
dropped, before it is answered, and fails only on an error that sync met. A
create, remove or attribute change is still answered before it is on the
drive, and is on it by the next COMMIT, stable write or rename.

The read-only nfsd parameter commit_is_durable says a kernel has this, and
vmproxy exports async only where it finds it.

